			<div class="content" id="post-content-4859288">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>A humanoid, resembling a male human, consisting of cells with anomalous biological characteristics</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm&#8230;concerned by this list. It makes this SCP idea feel like, super x-man-y: especially considering it's basically just, wolverine without adamantine claws/skeleton.</p>
<p>This feeling becomes stronger when I read your 'Central Narrative' and, instead of an explanation of what the actual SCP article is about or the story you're trying to tell about this dude, I just get more information about what the SCP does and how it hunts and what it wants. The final nail is driven into this coffin when the Hook/Attention grabbed section introduces even more anomalous properties and weird things this does.</p>
<p>At it's core: my issue with this pitch is that you're proposing something that sounds a lot more like your cool fighting OC or a DnD Monster than it does like you're proposing an interesting short story about a weird thing that the Foundation interacts with.</p>
<p>Like, my central question here is: once you've described this thing in the Description of your SCP article, what else are you doing with this? How are you intending to get a reader invested in this monster? How are you intending showing off how cool it is and how the various anomalous things it can do make life difficult for the Foundation? How do you want to make a reader feel?</p>
<p>My hot advice here: put this on the back burner for now. Go away, read a tonne of new SCPs, read a bunch of the guides on writing a narrative in SCPs (see e.g. <a href="/an-exercise-in-narrative-based-scps">An exercise in narrative based SCPs</a> or <a href="/yourjouney-part-2">The Journey of Your First SCP: Part II - Narratives &amp; Originality</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

