<p><strong>Beginning statement</strong></p>
<p>This is hard to understand, like extremely hard to understand. Everything I say may be wrong in your eyes, but, this is only to give you a basic review.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;me&nbsp;for&nbsp;the&nbsp;review</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;to&nbsp;hide&nbsp;the&nbsp;review</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>First of all, let me dissect your SCP</strong><br />
The SCP you described is very hard to understand. Your elevator pitch, (Which is supposed to tell me what the SCP is and what makes it anomalous) only touches on the SCP being a family man, and well respected, likely a dentist. The elevator pitch also describes how the SCP is found. Here's the problem, this is an SCP article not an SCP Story, and because you already have given me no clear description of the SCP, it's a big red flag.<br />
<strong>The Detective and the Story</strong><br />
Alright so already a problem with the logic of the detective is him getting PTSD from the cases he has been on, and you think he can have those bad thoughts removed by amnestics? There would be no way to dull the PTSD without a psychiatrist, (Thank god you at least did that). The story of this is nothing to special, not good, yet not bad, more or less cliche at worst.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<strong>Conclusion</strong><br />
You will need to add a lot to this, mostly fixing up the description of the SCP. I know it's not in the review but the Dentist seems to have no anomalous properties, so why the hell is it an SCP?
							</div>

								
					
